course: dictionary
lesson: 2814
title: Distraction
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A distraction is something that takes your attention away from what you should be doing. «Phones are a distraction in class» means phones pull your mind away from learning. Distraction can also mean something fun that takes your mind off worries, such as a game or bubbles.
explain: Read the context: «Distraction, such as counting, looking at a picture, blowing bubbles or squeezing a hand, helps the brain pay less attention to pain, and looking away is not cheating.» Here the sentence lists the things that take attention away, so distraction must mean something that moves your attention elsewhere.
explain: Some phrases use the word in special ways. «Driven to distraction» means so worried or confused that you can hardly think. «A welcome distraction» is a nice break from something hard. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A distraction is something that takes your attention away.
fact: «Distraction» can mean something fun that takes your mind off worries.
fact: «Without distraction» means with your full attention on one thing.
fact: «Driven to distraction» means so worried or confused that you cannot think clearly.
fact: Distractions can make learning hard, so teachers often remove them, and distraction while crossing roads or driving can cause accidents.
exercise: «Phones are a distraction in class.» What is a distraction here? | A type of paper | A cloud in the sky | *Something that takes your attention away from your work | A kind of sum
exercise: «Distraction, such as counting, looking at a picture or blowing bubbles, helps the brain pay less attention to pain.» What does distraction help with? | Writing faster | *Paying less attention to pain | Making a loud noise | Getting wet
exercise: After a hard test, Sam says a game with friends is a welcome distraction. What does he mean? | Being told to sit still | *A pleasant break that takes his mind off the test | Winning a prize for the test | Doing more tests
test: In the school subjects «Ambition, Distraction, Uglification and Derision», which sum does Distraction match? | Division | Addition | *Subtraction | Multiplication
test: Which phrase means someone has been made very worried or confused? | A welcome distraction | *Drive someone to distraction | Cause a distraction | Without distraction
exam: Ella says a distraction must always be bad. Is she right? | Yes, every distraction is bad and should be avoided | Yes, because distraction means a sum | *No, a distraction can be a fun break that takes your mind off worries | No, because distraction only means a cloud
